\section{Conjunctive average with surveys, \texttt{conjunctavgsurvey}}
\label{conjunctavgsurvey}

This module used to be \cref{conjunctavg} with one difference: the
assignment group could contain mandatory surveys, which had to be completed
but whose scores were ignored.
That difference is gone.
Every summary module now handles surveys (\cref{surveys}), and
[[conjunctavg]] applies exactly this rule (\cref{conjunctavg-surveys}).
A separate survey variant would only invite the question of which module a
group needs, and choosing the plain one for a group that holds a survey
silently made the result depend on how Canvas happened to score it.

The module stays, because scripts name it: grading scripts run
[[canvaslms results -S canvaslms.grades.conjunctavgsurvey]], and teachers'
own modules may import from it.
It is now an alias that re-exports everything from [[conjunctavg]]---including
[[is_survey]] and [[survey_completed]], which [[conjunctavg]] imports from
[[surveys]]---so the same functions are importable from here, and they are
the very same objects.

Importing it emits a [[DeprecationWarning]].
Python hides that category by default unless the code that triggered it runs
as [[__main__]], and with [[stacklevel=2]] the trigger is whoever imported the
alias.
For [[canvaslms results -S]] that is [[load_module]] in the [[results]]
command, so a normal run prints nothing extra and its tab-separated output
stays clean.
A teacher's own script that imports the alias directly is told once, which is
the audience the warning is for; so is anyone who asks to see deprecations,
under [[pytest]] or [[python -W default]].
<<[[conjunctavgsurvey.py]]>>=
"""
Deprecated alias of `canvaslms.grades.conjunctavg`, which handles surveys.

Use `canvaslms.grades.conjunctavg` instead: every summary module now
counts a survey (a classic Canvas quiz of survey type) as passed once it is
completed, see `canvaslms.grades.surveys`. This module re-exports
everything from `canvaslms.grades.conjunctavg`.
"""

import warnings

from canvaslms.grades.conjunctavg import *

warnings.warn(
  "canvaslms.grades.conjunctavgsurvey is deprecated, "
  "use canvaslms.grades.conjunctavg, which handles surveys",
  DeprecationWarning, stacklevel=2)
@

\subsection{What changed for groups summarized with this module}

The alias is not a perfect stand-in for the old module, because the old
module's notion of a survey was wrong.
It took every assignment that is not graded with letters for a survey, so it
differs from [[conjunctavg]] wherever that notion and a real survey part ways.
\begin{description}
\item[Points-graded work is graded work] A FeedbackFruits item graded out of
  100 points used to pass on mere submission.
  Now its grade is read like any other: an ungraded submission fails, and a
  numeric grade is an unknown grade, which raises an error that names it.
  The same goes for a New Quiz graded in points, since Canvas does not tell
  us whether a New Quiz is a survey.
\item[A survey is judged by completion only] The old module read a survey's
  grade first and only fell back to completion for grades it could not
  read, so a survey a teacher had marked \enquote{incomplete} failed and a
  letter-graded survey's A entered the average.
  Now a completed survey is a P and nothing else, whatever its grade.
\item[A letter-graded survey is a survey] Conversely, a classic survey graded
  with letters used to be graded work; now it counts by completion.
\end{description}

Let's verify that the alias is the same module, and pin the first of those
differences, the one that motivated the change.
The import silences the deprecation warning, which one test checks
explicitly, so that it does not clutter every test run.
<<test [[conjunctavgsurvey.py]]>>=
"""Tests for canvaslms.grades.conjunctavgsurvey, an alias of conjunctavg."""
import importlib
import warnings
import os
import subprocess
import sys

import pytest

import canvaslms
import canvaslms.grades.conjunctavg as conjunctavg
with warnings.catch_warnings():
  warnings.simplefilter("ignore", DeprecationWarning)
  import canvaslms.grades.conjunctavgsurvey as conjunctavgsurvey
import canvaslms.grades.surveys as surveys

<<test functions>>
@

The public functions are the same objects as in [[conjunctavg]], and the
survey predicates are the shared ones.
<<test functions>>=
def test_alias_exports_conjunctavg_functions():
  for name in ["summarize_group", "summarize", "missing_assignments",
               "is_passing_grade", "a2e_average", "normalize_grade"]:
    assert getattr(conjunctavgsurvey, name) is getattr(conjunctavg, name)
  assert conjunctavgsurvey.is_survey is surveys.is_survey
  assert conjunctavgsurvey.survey_completed is surveys.survey_completed
@

Same objects should give the same results; we check that on a group with an
exam, a completed survey and an uncompleted one, for both the summary and the
[[--missing]] report.
<<test functions>>=
def test_alias_gives_same_results(grading_objects):
  exam = grading_objects.graded(grading_objects.submission(
    "B", submitted_at="2026-01-10T10:00:00Z"), name="Exam")
  done = grading_objects.survey(grading_objects.submission(
    "50", submitted_at="2026-01-11T10:00:00Z"))
  undone = grading_objects.survey(None, name="Second survey")
  users = [grading_objects.user()]

  for group in [[exam, done], [exam, done, undone]]:
    assert list(conjunctavgsurvey.summarize_group(group, users)) \
      == list(conjunctavg.summarize_group(group, users))
    assert list(conjunctavgsurvey.missing_assignments(group, users)) \
      == list(conjunctavg.missing_assignments(group, users))
@

A FeedbackFruits item graded out of 100 points that the student submitted
but nobody has graded is not passed.
The old module took it for a survey and passed it.
<<test functions>>=
def test_submitted_ungraded_points_assignment_fails(grading_objects):
  user = grading_objects.user()
  peer_review = grading_objects.points(grading_objects.submission(
    None, submitted_at="2026-01-10T10:00:00Z"))

  grade, _, _ = conjunctavgsurvey.summarize(user, [peer_review])

  assert grade == "F"
@

The deprecation is visible to those who ask for it, as under [[pytest]], and
silent in a normal run.
We reload the module to see the warning again, since the import above has
already run it.
We check the normal run in a fresh interpreter, whose warning filters are
Python's defaults, loading the alias the way [[canvaslms results -S]] does.
<<test functions>>=
def test_alias_warns_about_deprecation():
  with pytest.warns(DeprecationWarning, match="canvaslms.grades.conjunctavg"):
    importlib.reload(conjunctavgsurvey)


def test_deprecation_silent_by_default(monkeypatch):
  src = os.path.dirname(canvaslms.__path__[0])
  monkeypatch.setenv("PYTHONPATH", src)
  monkeypatch.delenv("PYTHONWARNINGS", raising=False)
  run = subprocess.run(
    [sys.executable, "-c",
     "from canvaslms.cli.results import load_module; "
     "load_module('canvaslms.grades.conjunctavgsurvey')"],
    capture_output=True, text=True)

  assert run.returncode == 0
  assert run.stdout == ""
  assert run.stderr == ""
@
